toms and leukocytosis remain supportive for the broader myeloproliferative neoplasm category."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the hypercalcemia (Ca²⁺ 10.9 mEq/L) with no described lytic bone lesions or monoclonal gammopathy in the vignette support the lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorder hypothesis?", "summary": "Hypercalcemia without lytic lesions or monoclonal protein weakly supports plasma cell disorders but does not strongly confirm them."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the recent viral illness history (cold) and absence of persistent infection markers in the vignette support the leukemoid reaction hypothesis?", "summary": "The cold history weakly supports reactive leukocytosis, but normal differential disfavors leukemoid reaction."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) contradict the chronic myeloid leukemia hypothesis within the chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm category?", "summary": "Elevated LAP strongly contradicts CML, a representative disease of the chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm branch."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of lymphocytosis or circulating blasts in the differential disfavor the lymphoid neoplasm hypothesis within the lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorder category?", "summary": "Normal differential moderately disfavors lymphoid neoplasms like acute lymphoblastic lymphoma."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the hypercalcemia and subacute constitutional symptoms contradict the reactive leukocytosis hypothesis?", "summary": "Hypercalcemia weakly contradicts reactive leukocytosis, which typically lacks metabolic abnormalities like hypercalcemia."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.547], ["B3", 0.233], ["B2", 0.186]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch (B1) posterior (0.547) < 0.75; dangerous alternatives (B4, B5) remain unresolved with posterior >= 0.10 and high danger scores (0.9). Available discriminators (e.g., bone marrow biopsy, BCR-ABL testing) could further clarify myeloproliferative vs. blast-related disorders."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.82, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.5472036989538374, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'leukocytosis', 'constitutional symptom', 'hypercalcemia'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.547}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "posterior": 0.636, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=primekg]\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm: bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts"}
